# DuetSoftwareFramework packages from a separately published build of our
# own, installed in place of the Duet3D archive's.
#
# One line per package:
#
#   <package> sha256:<checksum of the published .deb> <https location>
#
# The DSF packages pin each other to one exact version and check it again at
# runtime, so a build of our own is installed as a whole: every package that
# pkg/build.sh --packages=progs,dwc,meta produces in the fork, at a version
# <dsf.version>+<suffix> (3.7.0~rc.2+mp.1 for a modified 3.7.0~rc.2).
# duetwebcontrol is among them: the build packages our fork of Duet Web
# Control, Meltingplot/DuetWebControl, at a version of the same form, and the
# meta package pins it. duetsd and reprapfirmware come from the archive, where
# the build's meta package pins them; reprapfirmware at dsf.rrf_version, which
# the meta package has to accept. An empty list installs the archive's
# packages. Only a published build is taken, checked against the pin: no local
# path, no fallback, a mismatch stops the build. See "Installing a
# DuetSoftwareFramework build of our own" in the README.
#
# A board firmware file is not a package of the build; it goes through
# firmware.list.

# 3.7.0+mp.10: upstream v3.7-dev at 3.7.0, which no longer rejects the skip
# keyword outside G-code files and reads the layer count, the filament used,
# the generator and the print time from the headers of more slicers (the
# filters are in config.json, which the image puts over the printer's copy on
# every boot), with the later upstream fixes for M997 S2, plugins and SPI,
# recover-duet, a tool for root that writes the firmware back to a mainboard
# waiting in its USB bootloader, the M99 that closes a macro, which DCS
# completes itself when the firmware aborts the file because newer firmware no
# longer replies to it, M122, which runs unbuffered so that no message box or
# macro request overtakes its report, M409, which DCS answers from its own
# copy of the object model once that is fully updated, passing only R1, array
# lengths, indexed keys and keys its copy cannot resolve on to the firmware,
# unbuffered codes, which hold back only the codes on their own stack level,
# so that an M409 sent while a macro runs on the same channel no longer
# deadlocks it, and unexpected empty replies, which DCS reports now instead
# of dropping them; plus our fix for a DCS crash on a non-finite expression
# result, our fix that keeps the patches coming for a model collection the
# firmware replaces, such as a board's drivers after an M999, the plugin
# services started together with DCS, so they come back when DCS is stopped
# and started again, and our work on the SPI link to the mainboard: a single
# corrupted exchange no longer resets the connection or puts both sides out
# of phase, a transfer may take up to 10 retries (MaxSbcRetries in
# config.json), the link recovers when the firmware restarts while DCS runs,
# DCS stops after 60 s without a complete transfer so that systemd starts it
# afresh, and it activates the link capabilities the firmware offers in
# limits.sbcLinkCapabilities, as the mainboard build in firmware.list does
# since 3.7.0+1.mp.6 (phase guard, sequence guard, extended link with response
# codes that tolerate bit errors), and counts its link recoveries in M122.
# When only DCS got bad data from the firmware, it exchanges the data again,
# as the firmware does, instead of restarting the full transfer; in the ESD
# test of 2026-11-03 four of five link events were such a bit error.
# M112 completes only once the emergency stop has reached the firmware, and
# our fixes for M20, M30, M36, M39, M111, M260/M261 with V, M472, M505,
# M581.1, M606 and M929 are in. The packages are built with root as owner of
# every file.
# Duet Web Control comes from our fork, 3.7.0+mp.6, the tag pkg/dwc-version
# in the fork pins: upstream 3.7.0 with the CHX 350 operator interface, which
# leaves machine actions such as preheating, homing and filament change to the
# flow macros of the printer configuration, reports a flow as completed only
# when it reaches its `echo ";; Completed"` line, which it keeps out of the
# notifications, forgets a flow answer once its box closes, locks the
# interface while the machine is starting, moves the axes while a job is
# paused, sets the bed, chamber and nozzle temperatures from their readings in
# the header, shows and sets the fans, warns of a stalled fan on every page and
# starts no job while one stands, lists the jobs on a USB stick at the panel
# and copies or starts them, shows the licence notices of both computers
# and history and analysis from the QualityAssurance plugin. The release of
# that Duet Web Control also carries the interface's SBC backend, which
# plugins.list pins at the same version.
# Duet3D has not published 3.7.0 yet, so the meta package accepts the firmware
# of 3.7.0-rc.2, the newest it has published (pkg/firmware-version in the
# fork), which dsf.rrf_version installs. The boards of the CHX 350 run the
# builds in firmware.list either way; from the package the image only uses the
# IAP binary of the mainboard update, which is the same in 3.7.0-rc.1 and
# 3.7.0-rc.2. Earlier builds: mp.9 of 3.7.0 restarted the full transfer when only
# DCS got bad data and carried Duet Web Control 3.7.0+mp.5, mp.8 of 3.7.0
# carried our own fix of that
# deadlock, which also held a code back behind unbuffered codes on deeper
# stack levels, and gave up a transfer after 3 retries, mp.7 held the codes of
# a running macro back behind such an M409, which waits for that macro, mp.6
# still passed M409 for most keys on to the firmware and carried Duet Web
# Control 3.7.0+mp.4, which mp.5 did too, mp.5 still cancelled that M99 and
# buffered M122, mp.4 also carried Duet Web Control 3.7.0+mp.3, mp.3 started
# the plugin services with DCS and carried Duet Web Control 3.7.0+mp.2, mp.2
# paired the meta package with the firmware of 3.7.0-rc.2, mp.1 accepted that
# of 3.7.0-rc.1, mp.9 of 3.7.0-rc.2 brought the root ownership, mp.8 the
# object model fix, packaged as the build user (1001), mp.7 carried Duet Web
# Control 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.6 and the object model changes of upstream, mp.6
# 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.5, mp.5 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.4, mp.4 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.3, mp.3
# 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.2, mp.9 of 3.7.0-rc.1 brought remote sensors on CAN expansion
# boards, mp.8 checked N line numbers in job and macro files, mp.7 RRF-style
# checksums on numbered lines, mp.6 kept the object model in step after an SPI
# transfer timeout.
